/**
 * Per-company business types, for the 626 companies whose source sub-industry is
 * a catch-all. Takes precedence over `SUB_INDUSTRY_TO_TYPE`.
 *
 * This is where the actual business knowledge lives. "Enterprise Software" told
 * us nothing about 203 companies and "Financial Services" nothing about 74, so
 * each was classified from what the company does — which vertical buys the
 * product, and whether the company carries risk, sells software, or sells human
 * effort. 568 of the 626 got a type; the remaining 58 are recorded in
 * `DELIBERATELY_UNTAGGED` below.
 *
 * Keyed by `${industry}||${name}`, not by name alone, and not by slug — the slug
 * is minted during the import that consumes this table.
 *
 * The industry belongs in the key for a reason that cost a debugging pass to
 * find: company names are NOT unique, and not even unique case-insensitively.
 * "STATS Perform" under Software & Internet and "Stats Perform" under Media &
 * Entertainment are different companies, and a case-insensitive name-only key
 * gave both the same business type — which the schema then rejected, because the
 * type belonged to the other industry. Scoping by industry also makes the
 * industry constraint structural rather than merely checked.
 *
 * Names are compared after collapsing whitespace, matching `cleanText`. The
 * source contains at least one non-breaking space inside a value, which is
 * invisible in a diff and produced a silent lookup miss until it was normalised
 * on both sides.
 *
 * Reviewed and vetted independently after assignment; see docs/business-types.md.
 */
